From 60a69ec5ae6d25adf4221ce3cd896c384eaba1fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 01:56:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/17] fix(mailbox): keep a mail identity from changing hands
 on username reuse
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Addresses were derived from the current username, and the alias table was
writable by any user. Both let a mail identity move between accounts.

A renamed account released its address: Gitea frees the old username and lets
the next account register it, so mail to the old address — password resets and
confirmations sent by third parties — was delivered to whoever claimed the name
next. Renaming now retains the old local-part as a "retired" assignment bound to
the original account, so it keeps reaching its original owner and an account
that later registers that username does not inherit it. AddressForUser reports
no address for such an account rather than claiming one it will not receive mail
at. Stored messages were always keyed by account, so nothing already delivered
was ever affected.

Address assignment moves from the user to administrators, at /-/admin/mailbox.
A user could previously claim any free local-part for themselves, which is the
same identity-transfer problem from the other direction; the mail settings page
now only reports which addresses reach the account. The new page lists every
assignment with its owner and origin, marking the ones a rename produced so an
administrator can release one deliberately.

With aliases no longer user-writable, the alias table becomes the authority for
address ownership and is consulted before the username. The previous ordering
existed only to stop a user-created alias from shadowing an account, a risk that
no longer exists.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Matheus Sampaio Queiroga <srherobrine20@gmail.com>
